        'Glossary' => [
            'body' => '<h4>IFRS 9 measurement</h4><ul><li><b>ECL, expected credit loss</b>. The provision: exposure multiplied by the probability of default and the loss given default, discounted where the discounted basis is used.</li><li><b>EAD, exposure at default</b>. The amount owed if the borrower defaults: the carrying amount plus any undrawn commitment multiplied by the utilisation rate.</li><li><b>PD, probability of default</b>. The chance of default, over twelve months for Stage 1 and over the remaining life for Stages 2 and 3.</li><li><b>LGD, loss given default</b>. The share of exposure not recovered after collateral and recoveries.</li><li><b>Stage 1</b>. Performing. Twelve-month expected credit loss.</li><li><b>Stage 2</b>. Underperforming after a significant increase in credit risk. Lifetime expected credit loss.</li><li><b>Stage 3</b>. Credit impaired, in default. Lifetime expected credit loss measured on the net carrying amount.</li><li><b>SICR, significant increase in credit risk</b>. The trigger that moves an exposure from Stage 1 to Stage 2.</li><li><b>DPD, days past due</b>. How long a payment has been outstanding. It drives the quantitative stage and the prudential class.</li><li><b>Amortised cost</b>. The carrying amount built up as opening balance plus effective interest less cash received.</li><li><b>Discounting effect</b>. The reduction in the provision that applying present value produces.</li><li><b>Coverage ratio</b>. Expected credit loss divided by exposure, shown as a percentage.</li></ul><h4>Models</h4><ul><li><b>Transition matrix</b>. The probabilities of moving between grades or stages over a period; the basis of the probability of default.</li><li><b>Transition profile</b>. The configuration that decides which data and grading columns build a matrix, and whether it counts loans or balances.</li><li><b>Cumulative matrix</b>. Monthly matrices chained together to give multi-period, and therefore lifetime, probabilities.</li><li><b>Internal grade</b>. The A to G master risk scale mapped from the twelve-month probability, where A is the lowest risk and G is default.</li><li><b>Cure rate</b>. The share of Stage 3 exposure restored to performing.</li><li><b>Recovery rate</b>. The share of defaulted exposure recovered.</li><li><b>CCF, credit conversion factor</b>. The share of an undrawn commitment expected to be drawn at default. The platform uses the facility utilisation rate.</li></ul><h4>Forward-looking</h4><ul><li><b>FLI, forward-looking information</b>. The macroeconomic adjustment that turns a historical probability into a forward-looking one.</li><li><b>Macro element</b>. An economic variable such as inflation or the exchange rate, held with its values by period.</li><li><b>Scenario weight</b>. The probability attached to the base, upside or downside scenario. The three must sum to one hundred per cent.</li><li><b>Regression</b>. A fitted relationship between historical credit loss and macro variables, used to convert a macro movement into a probability adjustment.</li><li><b>Overlay</b>. A management adjustment applied on top of the modelled result, recorded with its reason.</li></ul><h4>Interest and revenue</h4><ul><li><b>EIR, effective interest rate</b>. The rate that exactly discounts the contractual cash flows, including integral fees, to the initial net investment.</li><li><b>CIR, contractual interest rate</b>. The rate stated in the offer letter, before fees.</li><li><b>Integral fee</b>. A fee or cost directly attributable to originating the loan, and therefore part of the effective interest rate. Other fees are period income.</li><li><b>Unwind</b>. The interest recognised on the loss allowance of a Stage 3 exposure as time passes.</li><li><b>Readiness</b>. The set of named conditions a contract must satisfy before its effective interest rate can be solved.</li><li><b>Maker and checker</b>. A control where the person who records a decision cannot be the person who approves it.</li><li><b>Draft</b> and <b>Lock</b>. A draft can be changed; a locked record is final and needs a deliberate, recorded reopening.</li></ul><h4>Data and structure</h4><ul><li><b>Reporting period</b>. The month-end loan book snapshot, written as year and month, that every screen is scoped to.</li><li><b>Financial period</b>. The accounting period the organisation reports on, which can be open or closed.</li><li><b>Portfolio</b>. A segment of the book such as MAIIC core, FInES or Mega Farm, used by every stage table and roll-forward.</li><li><b>Sector</b>. The economic sector of the borrower, used for concentration reporting.</li><li><b>Product group</b>. The lending product family.</li><li><b>Collateral allocation</b>. Attaching a collateral item, at its discounted value, to a specific exposure.</li><li><b>Forced-sale discount</b>. The haircut applied to a collateral valuation to reflect what a quick sale would realise.</li><li><b>Extract A, B and C</b>. The core banking extracts: the contract master, the transactions and remaining schedules, and the general ledger interest postings.</li><li><b>Control total</b>. An account balance from the trial balance that all detail must reconcile to.</li><li><b>Golden number</b>. A fixed expected result pinned by a test so that any change to a calculation is noticed.</li></ul><h4>Prudential</h4><ul><li><b>RBM classification</b>. The Reserve Bank of Malawi prudential classes: Pass, Special Mention, Substandard, Doubtful and Loss.</li><li><b>NPL, non-performing loan</b>. Substandard, Doubtful and Loss taken together, ninety or more days past due.</li><li><b>HHI, Herfindahl-Hirschman index</b>. A concentration measure; above 2,500 counts as highly concentrated.</li><li><b>Regulatory reserve</b>. The excess of the prudential provision over the IFRS 9 provision, where the directive requires more.</li></ul><h4>Governance</h4><ul><li><b>Governance setting</b>. One calculation convention of the EIR engine, kept as a record with a fixed list of options, an effective date, a proposer and an approver rather than as a line of code.</li><li><b>Value in force</b>. The approved option that applies on a date: the latest approved change whose effective date is on or before it.</li><li><b>Effective date</b>. The first day a change applies. It must be later than the last approved change, so a change never restates an earlier month.</li><li><b>Maker-checker</b>. Two people for every change: one proposes, a different one approves. Used for EIR locks, fee classification and governance settings.</li><li><b>Superseded value</b>. A value replaced by a later approved change. It stays in the history with who replaced it and when, and it still governs the months before the change.</li></ul>',
        ],

        'Troubleshooting and FAQ' => [
            'body' => '<h4>Signing in</h4><ul><li><b>The security code is rejected.</b> Codes are single use and expire after a few minutes. Press the refresh button beside the image and type the new code. The code is not case sensitive.</li><li><b>The security code image does not appear.</b> Report it to ICT: the server component that draws it is unavailable.</li><li><b>My password is correct but sign-in fails.</b> Either the credentials do not match, in which case the page says so, or your account has been made inactive. Ask an administrator to check the active flag.</li><li><b>Too many attempts.</b> Repeated failures are throttled for a short period. Wait a minute and try again.</li></ul><h4>Seeing the right thing</h4><ul><li><b>A page shows no data.</b> Check the reporting period first. Most screens open on the latest period that has data, and a period with no calculation shows nothing rather than zeros.</li><li><b>I cannot see a menu item.</b> Your role does not carry that permission. Ask an administrator.</li><li><b>A modal says I do not have rights.</b> The same cause. The platform explains it rather than failing silently.</li><li><b>The session expired.</b> A modal explains it and offers the sign-in page. Sign in again; unsaved form input is lost, so save long entries as you go.</li></ul><h4>Figures</h4><ul><li><b>Two reports disagree.</b> Confirm both are on the same reporting period and the same scope. A portfolio-level run and a total-level run cover different populations.</li><li><b>Non-performing loans do not equal Stage 3.</b> They are different definitions. Stage 3 is the IFRS 9 credit-impaired population; the non-performing ratio is the prudential ninety-day classification. The IFRS 9 versus RBM report exists to explain the gap.</li><li><b>The dashboard compare shows enormous percentage moves.</b> Early periods hold partial books, so growth measured against them is naturally large. Compare against a recent full month.</li><li><b>Coverage jumped this month.</b> Look at the stage split first: a single large exposure moving to Stage 3 moves coverage more than any model change.</li></ul><h4>Calculations</h4><ul><li><b>An ECL calculation is already running.</b> Another run holds the lock for that period and scope. Wait for it to finish. If it will not clear, ask Dupleix to release the lock.</li><li><b>I cannot edit a transition matrix or a loss given default run.</b> It is locked or closed for audit integrity. Create a new draft instead.</li><li><b>An EIR contract is blocked.</b> Open Coverage and Blockers and read the named blocker. Each one says exactly what is missing, for example an unapproved schedule or an unreviewed fee line.</li><li><b>I cannot review a fee classification.</b> You classified it. A different person must review it, which is the control working as intended. Only an administrator can override that, and the override is recorded.</li><li><b>Records could not be discounted.</b> Those contracts have no approved and locked effective interest rate. Resolve them under EIR and Revenue Recognition, then run again.</li></ul><h4>Imports and files</h4><ul><li><b>My import stays at pending.</b> The background worker is not running. Ask ICT to start it; nothing is lost, the job resumes.</li><li><b>My import rejected rows.</b> Download the failed-rows file from Import History. Every rejected row carries its reason, usually a missing column or a value that is not a number. Fix the file and import again.</li><li><b>A PDF downloads blank.</b> Raise a support ticket naming the report and the period.</li><li><b>Excel shows numbers as text.</b> The exporter converts them back to real numbers; if it happens, report it with the file attached.</li></ul>',
        ],

        'Browser and printing notes' => [
            'body' => '<h4>Browsers</h4><p>The platform is built for current versions of Microsoft Edge, Google Chrome and Mozilla Firefox. It works on a laptop, and the layout narrows sensibly on a tablet; a phone is not a practical size for the wide tables. Keep your browser updated: an out-of-date browser is the usual cause of a page that renders oddly.</p><h4>Screen size and zoom</h4><p>Wide tables scroll sideways inside their own panel rather than stretching the page, so the sidebar and header stay put. If a table feels cramped, reduce the browser zoom with Ctrl and minus, or collapse the filter panel with <b>Hide filters</b>. On a narrow screen the sidebar becomes a drawer opened from the menu button.</p><h4>Printing</h4><p>Do not print a screen from the browser. Every report carries a <b>Download PDF</b> button that produces the branded document with the MAIIC logo, the tricolour bar, the generating user, the confidentiality note and page numbers. That is the version to circulate or file. The four System Documentation documents have the same button.</p><h4>Downloads</h4><p>CSV files open in Excel but hold plain rows. The <b>Excel</b> export produces a branded workbook where figures are real numbers and remain summable, so use it rather than the CSV when someone will do arithmetic on the result.</p><h4>Sessions</h4><p>You are signed out after a period of inactivity set by your administrator. Save long entries as you go. Two-factor authentication, if you have enabled it, is asked for at sign-in, not again during the session.</p><h4>Confidentiality</h4><p>Every screen and export holds real customer names and balances. Downloaded files carry the same sensitivity as the system itself: store them where client data belongs and do not forward them outside the approved distribution list on the cover of this manual.</p>',
